INT. COMMUNITY-GARDEN TOOL SHED - NIGHT

A cramped plank shed that smells of manure and linseed oil. A single bulb on a string. Rakes in racks. A calendar from 1998.

Five neighbors sit on buckets and overturned crates, an emergency meeting in a space not built for five.

CORNELIA "NELLIE" OKOYE-PRESCOTT (60s, gardening gloves, a loud cardigan) presides on a stool. Next to her: MARCUS TRAN-ABERNATHY (30s, a clipboard), BRIDGET KILLIAN-ADEYEMI (40s, bags under her eyes), OLD FELIX BRANDT-NJOKU (70s, a pipe unlit in his teeth), and young AISHA MORENO-VANCE (20s, still in her work apron).

NELLIE
The developers close the lot Friday.

MARCUS
They can't. We have the lease.

NELLIE
We had the lease.

MARCUS
Until when?

NELLIE
Until the thirtieth. Yesterday.

BRIDGET
Nobody told us.

NELLIE
The letter was in the mailbox of the man who died in March.

FELIX
(around his pipe)
That's Hendricks.

BRIDGET
Hendricks is dead, Felix.

FELIX
That's why the letter's late.

Silence. The bulb swings a quarter inch.

MARCUS
We can petition.

NELLIE
We have forty-two signatures.

MARCUS
Forty-two is a lot.

NELLIE
It's not enough.

AISHA
How many do we need?

NELLIE
Two hundred.

AISHA
By Friday?

NELLIE
By Friday.

BRIDGET
Then we pick up the pace. Door to door tomorrow.

MARCUS
I have a meeting.

BRIDGET
Cancel it.

MARCUS
I can't.

BRIDGET
Then you're out.

MARCUS
Out of what?

BRIDGET
The garden.

A pause so cold the bulb seems to dim. Marcus half rises. Felix sets his pipe on his knee. Aisha puts a hand on Marcus's sleeve. Bridget crosses her arms. Nellie lifts a rake from the rack and sets it on her lap.

NELLIE
Sit, Marcus.

He sits.

NELLIE (CONT'D)
Bridget, apologize.

BRIDGET
I will not.

NELLIE
Then explain.

BRIDGET
Hendricks's tomatoes are still in the south bed. He planted them. I water them.

FELIX
(gentle)
Every day.

BRIDGET
Every day. Because somebody has to. And if they bulldoze it Friday...

She doesn't finish. Marcus lowers his clipboard. Aisha stares at her hands. Felix lights his pipe, finally, with a slow match, and the shed fills with sweet smoke.

MARCUS
I'll cancel the meeting.

BRIDGET
Thank you.

MARCUS
Don't thank me. I'm taking the south side.

FELIX
Of course you are.
